1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
A hundred forty-two points with two averages, two standard deviations, and a correlation of minus point oh six.

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:10,910
Picture the cloud, and its best-fit line.

3
00:00:11,410 --> 00:00:12,940
Which way does it tilt?

4
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:18,950
You probably pictured a shapeless oval, with a nearly flat line.

5
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:20,930
Here are the real points.

6
00:00:22,030 --> 00:00:26,650
A dinosaur, drawn by Alberto Cairo, with these same five numbers.

7
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:35,920
In a review of seven hundred three physiology papers, eighty-six percent used bar graphs: an average and an error bar.

8
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:38,680
Only thirteen percent showed the points.

9
00:00:39,266 --> 00:00:41,246
How much can five numbers hide?

10
00:00:41,846 --> 00:00:47,476
Let's hide a star in them, and demand that all five match the dinosaur's to two decimals.

11
00:00:48,133 --> 00:00:52,253
Version one slides every point onto its own spot on a star.

12
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:55,830
We got the star, and lost all five numbers.

13
00:00:56,730 --> 00:01:02,040
The center moved, the x spread grew, and the y spread shrank by almost a quarter.

14
00:01:02,833 --> 00:01:05,523
So what exactly do five numbers pin down?

15
00:01:06,166 --> 00:01:10,386
The two averages mark a single point: the cloud's balance point.

16
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,900
The two standard deviations reach one deviation out on each side, and draw a box.

17
00:01:17,566 --> 00:01:19,856
Many ellipses touch all four walls.

18
00:01:20,056 --> 00:01:21,716
The correlation picks one.

19
00:01:22,316 --> 00:01:29,166
Correlation r means one standard deviation to the right, on average r standard deviations up.

20
00:01:29,666 --> 00:01:33,666
That's the best-fit line, through the middle of every vertical slice.

21
00:01:33,966 --> 00:01:39,176
At the wall, the slice is just the touch point, r times ess why above the center.

22
00:01:39,476 --> 00:01:42,106
For the dinosaur, it's just below the middle.

23
00:01:42,766 --> 00:01:45,646
That ellipse is everything the five numbers know.

24
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:50,020
Now replay version one, and watch the ellipse.

25
00:01:50,533 --> 00:01:51,863
How would you fix it?

26
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:55,760
Slide it until the centers meet.

27
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:58,180
Stretch it until the boxes match.

28
00:01:58,680 --> 00:02:03,270
Then tip it, a slight shear, until it touches the walls at the same height.

29
00:02:03,933 --> 00:02:07,043
All five match now, to every decimal place.

30
00:02:08,233 --> 00:02:10,983
But add a sixth number: the median of y.

31
00:02:11,283 --> 00:02:13,113
Our star misses it by six.

32
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:23,590
In 2017, Justin Matejka and George Fitzmaurice built a search that keeps these five numbers fixed while it nudges points toward a target.

33
00:02:24,233 --> 00:02:27,613
From the dinosaur they grew twelve shapes: this circle,

34
00:02:28,413 --> 00:02:29,273
this star,

35
00:02:30,073 --> 00:02:31,133
this bullseye,

36
00:02:31,933 --> 00:02:32,803
this X,

37
00:02:33,603 --> 00:02:34,813
and these stripes.

38
00:02:35,213 --> 00:02:38,463
Every one has this same ellipse, and a different median.

39
00:02:39,066 --> 00:02:44,516
Francis Anscombe made the point in 1973, with eleven points of his own.

40
00:02:45,166 --> 00:02:49,956
His correlation is point eight two, so the ellipse touches the right wall high up.

41
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:58,410
His best-fit line follows the same rule: one ess ex across, r ess why up, through the center and that touch point.

42
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,200
So the slope is rise, over run.

43
00:03:02,633 --> 00:03:07,793
All four of his datasets share these five numbers, so they share the ellipse and line.

44
00:03:08,093 --> 00:03:12,193
A trend, a curve, an outlier, and one point doing all the work.

45
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,330
Your line was exactly right: five numbers fix its slope, minus point one.

46
00:03:19,830 --> 00:03:24,790
Your cloud was wrong: a blur around this ellipse, all that five numbers can draw.

47
00:03:25,390 --> 00:03:29,330
Most of those papers used a bar graph, which draws only two.

48
00:03:29,930 --> 00:03:32,630
The dinosaur lived in everything they leave out.

